title: "On the Analytical Engine"
author:
  - name: "Grace Hopper"
    affiliation: "Harvard Computation Laboratory"
    url: "https://example.org/hopper"
    equal: true
    contribution: "Designed the study and wrote the analysis."
  - name: "Charles Babbage"
    affiliation:
      - "Harvard Computation Laboratory"
      - "Analytical Society"
    equal: true
    contribution: "Built the engine and ran the experiments."
date: 2026-03-09
bibliography: refs.bib
---

A worked publication page. It sets its own `author:`, a `date:` and a
`bibliography:`, so it carries its own byline and citations rather than the site's.

The `author:` here is the **structured** form: each entry carries a name plus the
things a byline needs to say about a person. Affiliations are
written as names rather than as indices into a separate list, and the superscript
numbers a reader sees are derived from first appearance — so the two authors here
share the number `1` because they typed the same institution, with no index to keep
in sync. The scalar spelling (`author: "Grace Hopper"`) stays valid and is what every
other page in this corpus still uses; `note.tmd` next door exercises the site-level
fallback.

Both authors declare a `contribution:`, so the page carries the generated appendix —
which is Author Contributions and nothing else since the academic-publishing cluster
(`doi:`, `venue:`, `award:`, `links:`, `acknowledgments:`) was retired on 2026-08-03.

Equal contribution is marked with `equal: true`, which prints the `*` in the byline
and the note under it [@lovelace1843].
